The hip joint is a **ball-and-socket synovial joint** between the femur and the pelvic girdle. A large, neat diagram should show the following (shown in a vertical section):

- the **head of the femur** (the ball), rounded and fitting into the socket;
- the **acetabulum** of the hip bone (pelvic girdle), the cup-shaped socket;
- the **neck** and **shaft of the femur** below the head;
- **articular cartilage** covering the surfaces of the head and of the acetabulum;
- the **synovial membrane** lining the joint capsule;
- the **synovial fluid** in the synovial cavity between the two cartilages;
- the **capsule** (capsular ligament) enclosing the joint;
- the **ligaments** joining the femur to the hip bone.

At least five of these parts should be correctly labelled with label lines touching the structures.
